# jsonpatch

A dependency-free JSON Pointer ([RFC 6901](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6901)) and
JSON Patch ([RFC 6902](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6902)) library and command-line tool
for Node.js.

## Library

```js
import { parsePointer, applyPatch, PatchError } from './jsonpatch.mjs';

parsePointer('/a~1b/~0c'); // ['a/b', '~c']

const doc = { b: 0 };
const result = applyPatch(doc, [
  { op: 'add', path: '/1', value: 5 },
  { op: 'test', path: '/b', value: 0 },
]);
// result: { '1': 5, b: 0 } - doc itself is unchanged

try {
  applyPatch(doc, [{ op: 'remove', path: '/nope' }]);
} catch (err) {
  err instanceof PatchError; // true
  err.message; // 'operation 0: path not found'
  err.index; // 0
}
```

- `parsePointer(pointer)` returns the decoded reference tokens. It throws a `TypeError`
  (`pointer must be a string`) for a non-string and a `SyntaxError` (`invalid pointer`) for
  an invalid pointer.
- `applyPatch(doc, patch)` returns a new document; it never changes `doc` or `patch`, and the
  result shares no object or array with them. Every operation is checked first, then applied in
  order. All failures are `PatchError`s with an `index` (the failing operation, or `null` when
  `patch` is not an array).

Error reasons: `not an object`, `bad op`, `bad path`, `bad from`, `missing value`,
`cannot remove root`, `cannot move into own child`, `path not found`, `from not found`,
`bad index`, `test failed`.

## Command line

```sh
node jsonpatch-cli.mjs <patch> [<doc>]
```

Reads the patch from `<patch>` and the document from `<doc>`. A missing `<doc>`, or `-`, means
standard input (only one of the two may be standard input). The result is printed as JSON with
two-space indentation.

```sh
echo '{"b":0}' | node jsonpatch-cli.mjs patch.json
node jsonpatch-cli.mjs patch.json doc.json > out.json
```

Inputs are UTF-8 (a leading BOM is ignored), at most 1 MiB and at most 1000 levels deep.

| Exit | Meaning |
| ---- | ------- |
| 0 | success |
| 1 | read error: `no such file`, `is a directory`, `too large`, `cannot read` |
| 2 | usage error |
| 3 | `invalid UTF-8`, `invalid JSON`, `too deep` |
| 4 | the patch failed (`Error: operation <i>: <reason>`) |

Errors are written to stderr as a single line, for example `Error: doc.json: invalid JSON`.

## Tests

```sh
npm test
```
# jsonpatch

A dependency-free JSON Pointer (RFC 6901) and JSON Patch (RFC 6902) library and command-line tool for Node.js.

## Library

```js
import { parsePointer, applyPatch, PatchError } from './jsonpatch.mjs';

parsePointer('/a~1b/~0c'); // ['a/b', '~c']

const doc = { a: [1, 2] };
const result = applyPatch(doc, [
  { op: 'add', path: '/a/-', value: 3 },
  { op: 'test', path: '/a/2', value: 3 },
]);
// result: { a: [1, 2, 3] }; doc is unchanged
```

- `parsePointer(pointer)` returns the decoded tokens. It throws `TypeError('pointer must be a string')` for a non-string and `SyntaxError('invalid pointer')` for a malformed pointer.
- `applyPatch(doc, patch)` returns a new document. It never changes `doc` or `patch`, and the result shares no object or array with them. Numbers (including `-0`) and member order are kept.
- On failure it throws a `PatchError` with a `message` such as `operation 2: test failed` and an `index` (the 0-based operation position, or `null` when the patch is not an array).

All operations are checked for form first (`not an object`, `bad op`, `bad path`, `bad from`, `missing value`, `cannot remove root`, `cannot move into own child`), then applied in order (`path not found`, `from not found`, `bad index`, `test failed`).

## Command line

```sh
node jsonpatch-cli.mjs <patch> [<doc>]
```

Reads the patch from `<patch>` and the document from `<doc>`. A missing `<doc>`, or an argument of `-`, means standard input (only one input may be standard input). The result is printed as JSON with 2-space indentation.

```sh
echo '{"a":1}' | node jsonpatch-cli.mjs patch.json
node jsonpatch-cli.mjs patch.json doc.json
```

Inputs are read as UTF-8 (a leading byte order mark is ignored) and may be at most 1048576 bytes and 1000 levels deep.

| Exit | Meaning | stderr |
| ---- | ------- | ------ |
| 0 | success | (nothing) |
| 1 | read error | `Error: <name>: no such file` / `is a directory` / `too large` / `cannot read` |
| 2 | wrong arguments | `Usage: node jsonpatch-cli.mjs <patch> [<doc>]` |
| 3 | bad input | `Error: <name>: invalid UTF-8` / `invalid JSON` / `too deep` |
| 4 | patch failed | `Error: operation <i>: <reason>` |

`<name>` is the argument as given, or `stdin` for standard input.

## Tests

```sh
npm test
```
# jsonpatch

A dependency-free JSON Pointer ([RFC 6901](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6901)) and
JSON Patch ([RFC 6902](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6902)) library and command-line tool
for Node.js (ES modules, built-ins only).

## Library

```js
import { parsePointer, applyPatch, PatchError } from './jsonpatch.mjs';

parsePointer('/a/~01/~10'); // ['a', '~1', '/0']

const doc = { a: 1, list: [1, 2] };
const result = applyPatch(doc, [
  { op: 'replace', path: '/a', value: 2 },
  { op: 'add', path: '/list/-', value: 3 },
  { op: 'test', path: '/list/2', value: 3 },
]);
// result: { a: 2, list: [1, 2, 3] }; doc is unchanged
```

- `parsePointer(pointer)` returns the decoded reference tokens. It throws a `TypeError`
  (`pointer must be a string`) for a non-string, and a `SyntaxError` (`invalid pointer`) for a
  malformed pointer.
- `applyPatch(doc, patch)` returns a new document. It never changes `doc` or `patch`, and the
  result shares no object or array with them. Numbers (including `-0`) and member order are kept.
- All operations are checked for form before any of them is applied. Failures throw a
  `PatchError` whose `message` is `operation <i>: <reason>` and whose `index` is `<i>`.
  A non-array patch throws `patch must be an array` with `index` `null`.

Reasons: `not an object`, `bad op`, `bad path`, `bad from`, `missing value`,
`cannot remove root`, `cannot move into own child`, `path not found`, `from not found`,
`bad index`, `test failed`.

## Command line

```sh
node jsonpatch-cli.mjs <patch> [<doc>]
```

Reads the patch from the file `<patch>` and the document from `<doc>`. A missing `<doc>`, or an
argument of `-`, means standard input (only one of the two may be standard input). On success it
prints the result as `JSON.stringify(result, null, 2)` and exits 0.

```sh
echo '{"a":1}' | node jsonpatch-cli.mjs patch.json
node jsonpatch-cli.mjs patch.json doc.json > result.json
```

Inputs are UTF-8 (a leading byte order mark is ignored), at most 1 MiB, and at most 1000 levels
deep. Errors print one line to stderr:

| Exit | Message |
